    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I hear you on the technical side, but I can't help but wonder if there's a gap in the \"polish\" or the fundamental rigor that comes from those top-tier institutions. I've seen cases where high scores in a technical test don't necessarily mean they can navigate the complex social and strategic landscapes we deal with here at Orbit. \n\nLooking at her profile again, do you think we're potentially overvaluing these scores? Or is there a real risk that someone from a vocational state school might struggle to meet the high-level intellectual and cultural standards we've established? I want to make sure we aren't just chasing numbers and missing a fundamental mismatch in caliber.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is a valid concern that a lack of a highly selective, broad-based academic setting might suggest a different approach to strategic thinking. The risk lies in assuming that a specific *type* of rigorous training (prestige institution) is the *only* path to high intellectual caliber. Instead of just looking at the *origin* of the knowledge, try to see how the *technical knowledge* can be immediately applied to abstract business challenges.\n\nYou might need to design a targeted, high-level behavioral or strategic challenge in the interview to specifically test those cross-context and \"polish\" skills you are concerned about. This will reveal if the high technical scores are truly backed by the necessary framework for high-stakes, complex internal navigation.",
